## Git store

A git object store that keeps repositories in S3 and reads them by byte range, written to be lifted into a project of its own. It is the foundation layer of the plan in https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2259: this directory holds the pieces the rest builds on. **Blot does not use it yet.**

Nothing in this directory may require anything outside it except Node built-ins and declared npm packages (`tests/standalone.js` checks). There is no `config`, no `models`, no Redis. Whatever is specific to a host (an S3 client, the bucket and prefix, the cache directory and size, the per-repository lock) is passed in.

## What is here

A repository is a set of immutable packs, each with its `.idx`, and a small manifest for each push. The packs and the manifests sit in an object store, keyed under a prefix the host chooses (`git/{blogID}/`):

```
manifests/<seq>.json     (seq zero-padded to 10 digits, so keys sort in order)
manifests/latest.json    (a hint at the newest seq, nothing more)
packs/pack-<40 hex>.pack
packs/pack-<40 hex>.idx
packs/pack-<40 hex>.rev  (written by receive)
```

- **`objectStore/memory.js`, `objectStore/s3.js`**: the object store interface (`head`, `read`, `get`, `stream`, `put`, `putIfAbsent`, `delete`, `list`, `id`; the contract is written at the top of `memory.js`). The S3 store uses only what Backblaze B2 also supports: plain and multipart PUT, ranged GET, HEAD, DELETE and LIST, plus one conditional write: `putIfAbsent(key, buffer)` resolves true if it created the key and false if the key already existed, and of several racing writers exactly one gets true. On S3 it is a PUT with `If-None-Match: *`; a 412 is false, a 409 (concurrent writers) is retried a few times and then thrown. Nothing else is conditional. A stream of any size is uploaded in parts, read only when an upload slot is free, so memory is about `partSize * concurrency`. The memory store is for specs and counts what it is asked.

- **`ranges.js`**: `readRanges(store, key, ranges)` merges ranges that are close together into one read and runs the reads in parallel with a limit.

- **`cache.js`**: one LRU cache on local disk across all repositories, capped by bytes. Nothing is pinned and nothing is required to be in it: an entry can vanish at any moment, `put` may decline to store something, and a miss costs round trips, never correctness. A damaged or half-written entry (each file has a CRC and length, and is written to a temporary file then renamed) is a miss. It works at any cap, including one too small to hold anything. It also hands out **scratch reservations**: `reserve(bytes)` takes bytes out of the same budget (evicting cached data), gives a directory to write into, fails with `NoRoomError` when the rest of the budget is already reserved, can be `extend`ed, and is `release`d when the push ends. One cache instance should own its directory.

- **`idx.js`**: finds an object in a pack by reading the version 2 `.idx` by range: the 1 KB fanout, then the slice of sorted object IDs and offsets sharing the first byte, both cached. Cold, that is two or three small reads; warm, none.

- **`pack.js`, `inflate.js`, `delta.js`**: read one pack entry by range (its header, then its zlib stream in growing reads), and apply deltas.

- **`reader.js`**: `createReader({ store, cache, packs })` reads objects by ID from the repository's live packs: whole (`read`) or as a stream of chunks (`stream`, for objects stored whole). Delta chains are resolved across packs (`OFS_DELTA` within a pack, `REF_DELTA` to an object in any pack, which is how packs completed by `index-pack --fix-thin` refer to what a push did not send). An object found in several packs is read from the newest. Concurrent lookups which need the same part of an index share one fetch. An index is checked once against the size in the manifest (a HEAD alongside its first fetch), and so is a pack (alongside its first read). Every object read by ID is hashed and compared with the ID asked for, so a flipped bit, a bad offset, a truncated pack or the wrong base is a `CorruptError`, never wrong bytes.

- **`manifest.js`**: the per-repository document: format version, sequence number, refs, the live pack list, a ref snapshot per push, and a pointer to the history pack. `read`, `parse`, `serialize`, `validate`, `advance` (the manifest after a push) and `swap`. Each push writes a new manifest to `manifests/<seq>.json`, which is never overwritten, and the repository is whatever its newest manifest says. **The conditional create of that object is what makes a swap safe**: `swap` calls `putIfAbsent` for `seq + 1`, and if it is false someone swapped first and `swap` throws `StaleManifestError`, writing nothing. A create which lands but loses its response is not mistaken for that: every manifest `advance` makes carries a random `writer` nonce, and when the create answers false or fails in a way which may have applied it (a network error, a timeout, a 5xx; not a 4xx), `swap` reads the manifest at that seq. Its own nonce means the swap landed and it resolves as a success (so does calling it again with the same `next`); another's means the race was lost; no manifest after an error means it did not land and the error is rethrown; and if the read fails too it throws `OutcomeUnknownError` (code `OUTCOME_UNKNOWN`), which a host must not turn into either a success or a failed push. The AWS SDK itself retries a PutObject that fails with a dropped connection, a timeout or a 5xx (three attempts by default), which is exactly how a retry can find the key already created and get a 412. However long a process stalls, it cannot replace a newer manifest, and no single object holds the only copy of a repository's refs. The host's lock (a function passed in) is **not** what makes it safe, because a lock can expire under a process that has stalled. `swap` still takes it, since it stops two pushes from the same base doing wasted work, and checks under it that the base is still the newest so the loser finds out early. It also checks that every pack the new manifest adds is already uploaded, so packs always go up before the manifest that names them. `manifests/latest.json` (`{ "seq": N }`) is written after a swap, best-effort and overwritten each time; a failure to write it does not fail the swap. It is only a hint. `read` GETs it, HEADs `seq + 1`, `seq + 2` and so on until one is missing, and GETs that manifest; if the hint is missing, corrupt, names a manifest which doesn't exist or is more than a few behind, it LISTs `manifests/` instead, and with no manifests at all it reads as empty with seq 0. A numbered manifest never changes, so its bytes are kept in memory by key (a few per store) and only the question of which number is newest goes to the store: the usual read is a GET of the hint, a HEAD, and a GET of the manifest unless this process wrote or already read it; a swap is two HEADs, the conditional PUT and the hint PUT. Old manifests are not deleted; that belongs with compaction. Every push adds a snapshot of the refs to the manifest, so an untrimmed manifest only grows; trimming snapshots belongs with compaction too. Until then the 64 MiB limit `read` applies is what stops a manifest becoming unreadable: `swap` checks the serialised size first and throws `ManifestTooLargeError` (code `MANIFEST_TOO_LARGE`), writing nothing, because a numbered manifest is immutable and one that `read` refused could never be replaced. The manifest is always read from the object store, never the pack cache, because only the store knows which manifest is newest.

- **`objects.js`, `rev.js`, `packscan.js`**: `objects.js` parses commits, trees and tags (`parseCommit`, `parseTree`, `parseTag`), and throws `CorruptError` for bytes that are not one. `rev.js` builds a pack's `.rev` file from its `.idx`. `packscan.js` walks an incoming pack file front to back and reports which delta bases it names but does not contain.

- **`receive.js`**: receiving a push. See below.

- **`treediff.js`, `materialize.js`, `folderSink.js`**: diffing two trees by range and bringing a folder from one commit to another. See below.

- **`layout.js`, `errors.js`, `limit.js`**: key names, error classes (each with a `code`), and a concurrency limiter.

## Receiving a push

`receive({ store, cache, lock, commands, pack, limit, sizeHint })` takes the ref updates a client sent and the pack that followed them, and either makes them the repository's new manifest or changes nothing. The steps are listed at the top of `receive.js`. The ones that are easy to get wrong:

- **The pack is scratch, and scratch is budget.** The incoming pack is written under a `cache.reserve` reservation, which grows as the pack arrives and again for what `index-pack` writes. A pack over `limit` is refused, and a cache with no room for it fails the push with `NoRoomError`'s message. The reservation is released, and its directory deleted, in every case before `receive` resolves.
- **A thin pack needs its bases.** `packscan.js` finds the `REF_DELTA` bases the pack does not contain; they are read from the store in a batch and written to scratch as loose objects, which `git index-pack --fix-thin --fsck-objects` reads as an alternate. `--fsck-objects` is git's check that every object is well formed. It is not `--strict`, which would also check that links resolve, against a repository that is not on disk. The links are checked in JS.
- **Connectivity only checks what the push adds.** Stored objects are closed under what they point at, so for each new commit its parents and tree must exist, and for a tree only the entries that differ from the same place in the commit's first parent are looked at (a merge compares with its first parent, a root commit checks every entry). A tree in the pack which no commit led to is checked whole. This relies on every stored object being closed, so the orphan sweep and the rewrite script must keep it true. Every ref update's new tip must exist, even when the push carries no objects.
- **A history piece is appended, not rebuilt.** A push that brings commits also uploads a pack of just those commits, listed in the manifest's `historyPieces`. The history pack is a different thing (`history` in the manifest) and is not touched; compaction merges the pieces into it. A reader of the manifest that does not know about pieces can ignore them.
- **Uploads come before the swap, and the swap alone decides.** The pack, its `.idx` and `.rev`, and the history piece are uploaded first (`manifest.swap` also checks they are there). A failed upload leaves the manifest as it was. The manifest is then read again and the commands' old values checked against it, so a push may be built on a manifest that has since moved (another push, one of Blot's commits) as long as the refs it names have not.

`receive` does not throw. It resolves with an `outcome`: `ok`, `refused` (nothing changed and retrying the same push will not help: a ref not allowed, a ref that moved, a bad pack, too large, missing objects), `failed` (nothing changed and retrying may help: no room in the cache, the object store would not take an upload; the `error` is for the host's log), or `unknown`. **`unknown` is a swap that may or may not have happened and could not be read back (`OutcomeUnknownError`). It must be reported to the client as neither a success nor a failed push.** The result also carries the report's `status`, `results` and `messages`, worded as `receivePack.js` words them, so the host's HTTP layer only has to frame them. The `unknown` messages tell the user to run `git fetch` before pushing again.

**Progress is streamed.** `receive` takes an optional `progress(message)` and calls it as each stage starts (checking the pack, fetching the files the push builds on, checking the new commits, saving to Blot, saved), in words for a blog owner. `sideband.js` (`createResponse(res, { capabilities, commands })`) turns that into the HTTP response as it happens: each message goes out on band 2 straight away, then the report on band 1 and the flush. A client that sent `quiet` (git does when stderr is not a terminal, unless `--progress`) gets no progress, one without `side-band-64k` gets only the plain report, and a stage silent for five seconds sends `0005\x01`, the empty packet git's own receive-pack uses to keep connections alive (the spec checks a real git accepts it). Once the first byte is out the HTTP status cannot change, so every ending is a report: `finish(result)` for a result, `fail(err)` for anything else, which reports every ref as failed and tells the user to run `git fetch` before pushing again.

The refusals are `receivePack.js`'s: only master may be a branch, master cannot be deleted, and tags are kept. The host still owes the rest of what `receivePack.js` does around this: parsing the request, authentication, `validateTree` (submodules, symlinks, file modes), the follow-up sync alarm and the after-push callback.

## Blot's own commits

Blot's own writes (a preview file, a template file) are one commit, one pack and one manifest swap each. Grouping several writes into one commit, pack or swap was considered and rejected on 2 Nov 2026. Writes are infrequent and small, setup does not use `client.write` because `create.js` makes its own batches, and the one slow case is a large dashboard upload, which compaction and manifest pruning clean up afterwards. Do not add grouping without new evidence that writes are frequent or large.

## Materializing a folder

`materialize({ reader, from, to, sink, concurrency, maxSize, symlinks, log })` brings a folder from one commit to another and resolves with `{ written, removed, skipped }`. `from` and `to` are commit IDs, or null for the empty tree. `treediff.js` (`diffTrees`, an async generator) finds the changes: it reads the two trees by range and skips a subtree whose ID is the same on both sides without reading it, so the cost follows the size of the change. It holds one directory's entries and a few of the directories below it, never a listing of the repository. Each change is `{ path, oldMode, oldOid, newMode, newOid, valid }`. Blobs go to the sink through `reader.stream`, up to `concurrency` (default 8) at a time, and are checked against their ID as they go. `materialize.js` documents the rules at its top. They are the rules of the materializer on EBS (`app/clients/git/materialize.js`), so the same commits give the same folder: only 100644 and 100755 files are written, raw and without `.gitattributes` filters; a link or submodule is never written, but a file or link it replaced is removed; a path with an empty, `.` or `..` name, a `.git` name in any case, or a name that is not UTF-8 is skipped and logged; names are kept byte for byte, with no Unicode normalization. `symlinks: true` is the one addition, for a sink that wants links written, and Blot does not use it.

**The order is one a sink can apply as it goes.** Within a directory everything that goes away comes before anything that comes in, so a file that becomes a directory (or the reverse) and two names that differ only by case are safe. `materialize` also never runs a removal while a write is in flight, or a write while a removal is, which settles the cases that span directories on a case-insensitive folder.

**A blob stored as a delta is resolved in memory.** `reader.stream` can only stream an object stored whole. A delta goes through `read` with the reader's size cap (`maxSize`, 64 MB by default, or the option here), and a blob over it fails the whole call with a `TooLargeError` that names the path and the blob. It never writes a wrong or partial file. Git usually stores the newest version of a large file whole, so this is mostly a large file as it was before a later edit.

**A failure part way leaves the sink partly applied, and the host runs the same range again.** `materialize` stops starting work at the first failure, waits for what is running, and throws it. Running the range again is idempotent: a write replaces the file, a removal of something already gone does nothing, so it ends where an uninterrupted run would, however far the first got. The host records the last applied commit only after `materialize` resolves, and the store does not record it.

### Sinks

A sink has `write(path, stream, { mode, size })`, `remove(path)` and `symlink(path, target)`. Paths are relative with `/` between names and are already safe; `mode` is `0o644` or `0o755`; the stream is a Readable of exactly `size` bytes. `folderSink.js` (`createFolderSink({ root })`) is the local folder: it replaces a link or directory in the way, never follows a link, makes directories as needed, removes the directories a removal leaves empty, and writes a file to a temporary name before renaming it into place.

An S3 sink (`{blogID}/folder/` through `app/storage/folder`, later) has to do the same things its way:

- **Do not publish a file until its stream has ended without error.** The stream throws at its end if the bytes are not the blob, so an upload must be aborted (a multipart upload aborted, a single PUT never completed) rather than finished when the stream errors. A key may only ever hold a whole, checked file.
- **`write` replaces.** A PUT over an existing key is that. There are no directories to make, and none to remove, so `remove` is a DELETE and an absent key is not an error.
- **A path that was a file and is now a directory** (or the reverse) is two different sets of keys, so there is nothing to replace; `materialize` removes the old keys first.
- **Be safe to run twice, and to call concurrently,** up to `concurrency` writes at once. Never be asked for a removal and a write together.
- The mode is metadata the sink may keep (`x-amz-meta-mode`, say) or ignore, as long as the executable bit comes back out the same.

## Where an entry ends

The `.idx` gives where an entry starts, not where it ends. There are two ways to find the end: a `.rev` file (the pack's offsets in order), or inflating the entry and seeing where its zlib stream stops. This store inflates.

- Reading an object means inflating its entry anyway, so the end costs nothing extra. A `.rev` lookup would be a binary search of extra ranged reads per object, since it maps pack order to index order and not the other way round.
- Inflating checks the entry as it goes: zlib verifies the stream's checksum, and the output must match the size in the entry header exactly. That is how truncated and damaged data is caught.
- It needs nothing but the `.pack` and `.idx`. A `.rev` is only written by newer git, or by an option of `index-pack`, and Blot's own commits would have to write one too.

The cost is that finding an entry's end without wanting its data (to copy its compressed bytes verbatim into a clone, say) means inflating it and discarding the output. A pass over a whole pack for that is CPU, not memory or round trips. For that case receive writes a `.rev` beside each pack it uploads, so streaming a clone can use it; nothing in the reader does.

## Memory

Reads are bounded. Ranged reads are at most the caller's range (merged reads are capped at 8 MB). An entry is inflated in pieces and the input is only fetched as zlib asks for it. Objects stored whole can be streamed in chunks of about 16 KB. An object that is a delta is resolved in memory, so `maxSize` (default 64 MB, per call or per reader) caps the base, the deltas and the result, and a bigger object is a `TooLargeError`.

## Specs

`tests/` builds repositories with real `git` (delta chains, `OFS_DELTA` and `REF_DELTA` packs, a thin pack completed with `index-pack --fix-thin`, objects in several packs, a tag) and compares every object read through the store with `git cat-file`. `tests/helpers/faults.js` wraps any object store to fail on a schedule (an error after the write landed or before it, a truncated body, a delay, a stale answer), and `tests/faults.js` uses it for ambiguous swaps, cut-short reads, a push which dies between its uploads and a stale hint. `tests/receive.js` pushes with a real `git push` (through the small smart-HTTP server in `tests/helpers/receiveServer.js`) and calls `receive` directly with packs built by git, against the memory store and the fault-injecting one; it asserts the request counts of a one-file push. `tests/s3.js` runs the S3 store against the MinIO the test runner starts (`.github/actions/start-minio`); without `BLOT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT` it is skipped and says so. `tests/treediff.js` and `tests/materialize.js` build history by plumbing (`tests/helpers/repo.js`) and compare every diff with `git diff-tree` and every folder with `git checkout`, for the cases in the materializer on EBS's specs; `materialize.js` also checks the request counts of a one-file change, a re-run after a failure, and the memory of a 48 MB blob in a process of its own. The cache specs run at caps of a few hundred bytes.

## Not here yet

Streaming clone and fetch, Blot's own commits, compaction (and so the history pack itself, and `.rev` files for packs compaction writes), and the rewrite script. They build on the pieces above. Cache keys include the object store's `id`, so one cache can serve many repositories.
